<Docs
  title="Mental model"
  description="Projects, tasks, worktrees, terminals and reviews: what each object owns, and what happens when you quit."
  toc={toc}
>
  <p>
    Shepherd is one window over persistent local projects. A task is not just a chat: it is the
    durable record of one agent conversation, the worktree it edited and the review that follows.
    Keeping those boundaries clear makes parallel work predictable.
  </p>

  <h2 id="projects">Projects</h2>
  <p>
    A project is a local directory you added, usually a Git repository. Shepherd remembers its path,
    Git metadata, last active task and per-project terminal layout. A missing or moved directory is
    reported honestly; deleting a project from Shepherd never deletes its files, worktrees or
    branches.
  </p>

  <h2 id="tasks">Tasks</h2>
  <p>
    A task owns one conversation with one agent, in its own worktree. It carries a state, transcript,
    workspace layout and review data. Switching tasks swaps the chat and task-specific content; the
    inspector's selected tab and project-shared terminals stay where they are.
  </p>
  <TableScroll label="Task states">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>State</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{states.map((entry) => <tr><td><code>{entry.state}</code></td><td>{entry.meaning}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
    </table>
  </TableScroll>
  <p>
    The state comes from adapter events and process exit, not polling. A task moves to
    <strong>waiting</strong> when an approval or question is pending, and to <strong>review</strong>
    only when a completed turn actually changed the code.
  </p>

  <h2 id="worktrees">Worktrees</h2>
  <p>
    Each task gets a Git worktree and a branch from the project's default branch. The default path is
    <code>&lt;git-dir&gt;/shepherd/worktrees/&lt;slug&gt;</code> on branch
    <code>shepherd/&lt;slug&gt;</code>, outside the working tree, so it needs no ignore rule.
    Repositories with a <code>.git</code> file (worktrees and submodules) are handled.
  </p>
  <p>
    A shared checkout is an explicit opt-in with a warning. Dirty worktrees are never deleted:
    removing a task refuses when changes exist. Archived worktrees unused for 30 days are removed
    automatically, but dirty ones are skipped; reopening recreates a worktree from the branch or the
    recorded tip.
  </p>

  <h2 id="inspector">The inspector</h2>
  <p>
    Every task workspace has a required chat region and an always-on inspector with five tabs:
    <strong>Terminals</strong>, <strong>Preview</strong>, <strong>Review</strong>,
    <strong>Git</strong> and <strong>Files</strong>. Regions are fixed; panes resize inside their
    region and terminals split only inside the Terminals tab.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Project-shared terminal</strong> — visible in every task of the project and keeps its PTY across task switches.</li>
    <li><strong>Task terminal</strong> — belongs to one task, keeps running while another task is active, and reappears when its task is selected.</li>
    <li><strong>Closing a terminal view</strong> — detaches the view only; the PTY keeps running and can be reopened.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The terminal layout, split sizes and slot scopes are saved per project. Presets are starting
    points, not separate layouts per task.
  </p>

  <h2 id="approvals">Approvals</h2>
  <p>
    The default policy is <strong>agent-judged</strong>. Safe reads and a visible local command list
    are auto-allowed. Everything else goes to the agent's own review mode when the adapter exposes
    one; if no judge is available, it becomes a normal approval card and waits. It is never silently
    allowed.
  </p>
  <p>
    A card shows the tool, its arguments and the affected diff when one is available. Decisions
    record their source: safe list, agent or user. Projects may tighten the policy but cannot loosen
    it. Session and project grants can be reviewed and reset in Settings → Projects.
  </p>

  <h2 id="review">Review</h2>
  <p>
    A completed turn enters review only when HEAD, the branch or the worktree status/diff changed
    since the turn started. An edit that nets to zero, or a chat-only answer, returns the task to
    ready. Review is not a separate inbox: the task row and Review tab carry the badge, and the
    status bar and dock badge show the count.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Mark reviewed and archive</strong> keeps the branch and moves the task to done.
    <strong>Send review note</strong> starts a new turn with the note and the changed files.
    <strong>Squash-merge into base</strong> is available from the Git tab when the root is clean and
    on the base branch.
  </p>

  <h2 id="quit">Quit and resume</h2>
  <p>
    Quitting is quitting. If a task is working or waiting, Shepherd asks first and names what will
    stop. It then cancels turns where supported, closes agent input, terminates process groups, ends
    PTYs and marks working tasks interrupted.
  </p>
  <p>
    On the next launch, task workspaces restore, interrupted tasks offer <strong>Resume</strong>, and
    terminals appear as <strong>ended</strong> with their last working directory and command. Nothing
    claims to have survived that did not. If the app crashed, recorded process IDs are offered for
    termination only when their identity still matches.
  </p>

  <Callout tone="note" title="What is not here">
    There is no daemon, background service, second window or remote sandbox in v1. Shepherd is not a
    code editor, LSP host or debugger; files are viewable and diffs reviewable. Agent orchestration
    teams and the quota-aware pool are next, not current.
  </Callout>
</Docs>
